{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310719,"nonce":"IgElQzhCgTRMytOQW2Fy+stuIR0=","title":"SOLVED 2d1f037e (conformance 425-\u003e427). StdTypes typedefs were never lowered (pre_register swallowed Typedef), so Iterable/Iterator had NO ...","message":"**`Iterable\u003cT\u003e`/`Iterator\u003cT\u003e` resolve to Dynamic because their typedef targets are\nnever lowered.** The symbols exist; the anonymous structure `StdTypes.hx:116/145`\ndeclares does not.\n\n`compilation.rs:2514` states the design outright:\n\n\u003e \"Pre-register type declarations from default stdlib files (e.g. StdTypes.hx).\n\u003e This is lightweight: it parses the files and registers enum/class symbols into\n\u003e the symbol table **without full TAST lowering**, preserving lazy stdlib\n\u003e performance.\"\n\nSo `typedef Iterable\u003cT\u003e = { function iterator():Iterator\u003cT\u003e; }` mints a symbol and\nnothing else. `resolve_type_alias` (`tast/core.rs:1241`) then finds no TypeAlias\ntype for it and returns Dynamic.\n\n\n- `var i = it.iterator(); i.hasNext()` → **E0801 ambiguous dispatch**\n  (`ArrayIterator` vs `ArrayKeyValueIterator`). Annotating `var i:Iterator\u003cInt\u003e`\n  works, which is what makes it look like an inference bug.\n- **`Lambda` does not compile** (~10 conformance cases). `empty()` is\n  `return !it.iterator().hasNext();` — the same chain, nothing to annotate.\n- `for (x in { iterator: ... })` → **0**, silently: `Anonymous` is missing from\n  `is_iterator_class` (`stmt/for_in.rs:184`) so it falls to the array lowering.\n\n\n```\n[TYPELOWER] name=Iterable kind=Class     sym=SymbolId(10)   \u003c- before any fix\n[TYPELOWER] name=Iterable kind=TypeAlias sym=SymbolId(10)   \u003c- after fix A\n[STRUCT]  m=iterator step=0 kind=TypeAlias(10)\n[STRUCT]  m=iterator step=1 kind=Dynamic(15)   \u003c- the alias points at NOTHING\n[MCALL]   method=hasNext typed=false recv_kind=Dynamic(15)\n```\n\n1. `registration.rs:487-513` pre-registers a typedef with\n   `create_class_in_scope(...) // Reuse class for typedefs`, and returns early at\n   :491 when the name already exists.\n2. `lower_typedef_declaration` (`decls/mod.rs:453`) *does* build\n   `TypeAlias -\u003e \u003clowered target\u003e` and set it (:550-561) — but it never runs for\n   StdTypes, per the pre-registration note above.\n3. So the `SymbolKind::TypeAlias` arm (`types/mod.rs:225-264`), which resolves\n   targets correctly, is unreachable; the `Class` arm at :212 runs instead.\n4. `infer_method_call_return_type` (`expr/calls.rs:2095`) walks to a Class symbol,\n   `resolve_class_method_symbol` finds no members, and the return decays to Dynamic.\n\n\nA. `create_type_alias_in_scope` at registration.rs:503 — **DOES take effect**\n   (kind becomes TypeAlias, proven above), but **regresses `s_gen`** from a silent\n   0 to a trap stub and fixes nothing, because the target is still absent.\nB. `structural_method_return_type` reading the anon field's `Function` return —\n   never reaches an `Anonymous`; stops at Dynamic (trace above).\nC. Alias recovery by name — `find_typedef_anonymous_target_by_qname`\n   (`resolve.rs:480`) matches ONLY `qualified_name`, and these are root-level. A\n   bare-name variant finds nothing either: no TypeAlias entry exists.\nD. Resolving a placeholder Class through a same-named alias — same reason.\nE. Removing the negative cache in `resolve_type_alias` (core.rs:1261-1264, which\n   caches the Dynamic fallback permanently) — correct in principle, no effect\n   here: the Dynamic is a real resolution, not a cached one.\nF. Resolving the deferred alias target instead of the hardcoded\n   `dynamic_type()` at `types/mod.rs:696` — no effect, same reason as E.\n\n\nThe typedef TARGETS for StdTypes have to be lowered. That is a deliberate\nomission for cold-start, which is the subject of a whole family of work\n([[perf-stdlib-cache-shares-when-manifest-loaded]],\n[[project-stdlib-snapshot-embed-via-cli-buildrs]],\n[[feedback-snapshot-is-setup-phase-map-dont-build]]), so measure cold start, not\njust conformance.\n\nNote the BLADE manifest path DOES do this properly:\n`register_type_alias_from_blade` (`compilation.rs:2518`) builds a real\n`TypeAlias` and `resolve_blade_type` (:2679) handles `BladeType::Anonymous`. So\nlowering only StdTypes' few typedef targets in the non-manifest path may be\nenough and cheap — that is the first thing to try.\n\nFrom the oracle, worth knowing: **haxe REJECTS `for (x in dynamic)`** (\"You can't\niterate on a Dynamic value, please specify Iterator or Iterable\") but ALLOWS\n`d.iterator()` on a Dynamic and dispatches at run time. So E0801 refuses a\nprogram haxe accepts.\n\nThe iteration-handle machinery (`9923e279`, `b903f8e8`) fixes five other shapes\nwithout needing any of this — see [[bugs-increment-store-back-targets]].\n\n\n\n`pre_register_and_enums_from_source` (`compilation.rs` ~:5950) matches only\n`Enum` and `Abstract`; **`_ =\u003e {}` swallows `Typedef`**. That is why StdTypes'\nfour typedefs never lower while typedefs from other files do — a probe in\n`lower_typedef_declaration` shows `Bytes`, `IMap`, `Map`, `PosInfos`, `__Int64`\nlowering (PosInfos with an Anonymous target) and no Iterator/Iterable.\n\nAdding\n\n```rust\nparser::TypeDeclaration::Typedef(typedef_decl) =\u003e {\n    let _ = lowering.lower_typedef_declaration(typedef_decl);\n}\n```\n\n**works** — the probe then shows\n`[TYPEDEF] lowering Iterable sym=SymbolId(10) target_kind=Anonymous`, and\n`s_gen` stays at 0 with no regression.\n\n**But it turns two clean compile errors into worse outcomes**, so it must not\nland alone:\n\n| case | before | with the typedef fix |\n|------|--------|----------------------|\n| `var i = it.iterator(); i.hasNext()` | E0801 error | **exit 139 SIGSEGV** |\n| `Lambda.count([1,2,3])` | module fails to compile | **exit 0, prints 0** |\n\nMIR for the first (`RAYZOR_DUMP_MIR`, fn `Main.c`):\n```\n$16 = call_indirect $15($12)   \u003c- iterator() DOES go through the handle\n$44 = call fn160002($40)       \u003c- hasNext: DIRECT call to a bodyless declaration\n$49 = call fn160003($40)       \u003c- next:    same\n```\nSo `iterator()` is intercepted and `hasNext`/`next` are not. Tried and did NOT\nfix it: moving `try_iter_handle_method_call` ahead of `try_resolved_function_call`;\nrecognising the protocol structurally in `iter_protocol_of` (matching an\n`Anonymous` carrying `hasNext`+`next` / `iterator`, and following a Class symbol\nthrough `resolve_type_alias`). The receiver of the failing calls still does not\nreach the intercept — that is the next thing to instrument.\n\nLambda's 0 is the generic case: its `count` takes `Iterable\u003cA\u003e`, and the for-in\nhandle path deliberately declines inside a generic template (removing that guard\nmade the template fail to lower and `main` cascade into a trap stub).\n\n**So a complete change is: lower the typedefs AND make structural\n`hasNext`/`next` dispatch reach the handle AND handle the generic template.**\nAll three, or none — the first alone is a regression.\n\n\n\nFive coupled changes; none of them works alone, which is why four earlier\nsingle-change attempts all failed:\n\n1. `compilation.rs` ~5950 — `pre_register_and_enums_from_source` matched only\n   Enum/Abstract; `_ =\u003e {}` swallowed `Typedef`. Lower it, and the alias finally\n   has its `Anonymous` target.\n2. `expr/calls.rs` `infer_method_call_return_type` — read the method's signature\n   off the anonymous structure (a structural type declares methods as fields\n   whose type IS the signature). Fixes the inferred `var i = it.iterator()`.\n3. `iter_handle.rs` `iter_protocol_of` — recognise a protocol by the MEMBERS a\n   structure declares, not only by a symbol's name: what `iterator()` returns is\n   the structure itself, which carries no name.\n4. `iter_handle.rs` — drop the \"mentions a type parameter\" guard at the call, the\n   loop AND the coercion. The entry points come from the handle, not the element\n   type, so one handle serves a template and an instantiation alike. The tag\n   check still protects a receiver that carries none.\n5. `cranelift_backend.rs` — the generic-template stub (~:804/:1096) and the\n   `failed_funcs` pre-populate (~:795) refused any function whose DECLARATION is\n   generic. `Lambda.count` is 19 blocks of ordinary loop once iteration is\n   type-erased. Both now ask `references_type_params(function)` — does the BODY\n   still mention one — instead.\n6. `signatures.rs` — an imported callee keeps its param types in the context that\n   compiled it; all this one holds is a per-param NAME, already present in\n   `external_function_param_iface_names` (BLADE records `\\x08Iterable`). The\n   existing imported-callee fallback now recognises the protocols there, matching\n   the LAST DOT-SEGMENT for equality so `haxe.iterators.ArrayIterator` — a\n   concrete class — is never wrapped.\n\n**Issue6480 is NOT a regression from this work.** It is nondeterministic and\npre-existing: 30 harness runs gave 26 PASS / 4 WRONG_ANSWER with the detail\nvarying between \"1 != -1\" and \"2 != 0\". `haxe_box_bool_ptr` yields a heap pointer\nthat is then read as a `bool`, so the low byte decides. I blamed my own change\nfor it before checking determinism — the same error made earlier with Issue3635.\n**Test determinism BEFORE assigning blame.**\n\n\n`Iterator\u003cInt\u003e` in the type table is `TypeKind::Class { symbol_id: Iterator,\ntype_args: [Int] }` (types/mod.rs `SymbolKind::Class` arm — the pre-registered\ntypedef keeps that symbol kind), NOT a `TypeAlias` node. The alias's DECLARATION\n(`symbol.type_id`) is the `TypeAlias` whose `type_args` are the parameter nodes,\nso the positional zip is (declaration params) x (Class node args). Code that\nonly reads `TypeAlias` nodes (my first attempt at `alias_bindings`) never fires —\n`RAYZOR_STRUCT_TRACE=1` prints `class -\u003e alias` and the return stays `T`.\nFixed in `calls.rs` `structural_method_return_type` (both arms bind); see\n[[bugs-stdlib-silent-wrong-answers-eight-defects]] D4.\n\n- Index summary moved from MEMORY.md (2026-10-18): SOLVED 2d1f037e (typedef targets lowered, 5 coupled changes). TRAP: `Iterator\u003cInt\u003e` is a `Class{Iterator,[Int]}` NODE not a TypeAlias — bind alias args on the Class arm too (`RAYZOR_STRUCT_TRACE=1`).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_structural_typedefs_are_class_symbols` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}